1. Client context
Brightwell Home & Appliance is a fictional UK retailer of home appliances with 38 stores, a website, and delivery, installation, warranty and repair services. It has about 1,200 employees and about 420,000 customers. Its systems were added over time (stores, online sales, an acquired repair company, a warranty plan), so customer data now sits in seven unconnected applications.
2. Business problem statement
Brightwell's customer, sales, warranty and service data is held in seven systems that do not share a common customer identity or agreed business definitions. As a result, leadership receives different answers to basic questions such as "how many active customers do we have?" and "what was last month's revenue?". Finance spends about four days each month reconciling reports, marketing sends warranty offers to customers who already hold a warranty, and support agents open several systems to answer one call (average nine minutes). The board wants an AI assistant, but the CIO is not confident the underlying data is reliable enough to support it. Brightwell needs one trusted, governed view of customers, transactions and service activity, with agreed metric definitions, so that reporting is consistent, staff stop duplicating work, and AI initiatives can be built on data that is ready for them.
Impact figures above come from the fictional leadership interviews. ✏️ In discovery we would try to quantify each one in money (for example, four finance days per month, repeat marketing sends, minutes per call).
3. Symptoms and hypothesised root causes
Stakeholder	Symptom	Hypothesised root cause	How we would test it
CEO	Three different "active customer" numbers	No shared customer ID across systems, and no single agreed definition of "active"	Ask each team for their definition and query; compare counts on the same date
CFO	Sales dashboard revenue differs from ERP	Either missing data in one system, or different definitions (gross vs net, order date vs payment date, refunds) or load timing	Reconcile one month line by line and categorise each difference
Marketing Director	Warranty offers sent to existing warranty holders	Warranty data lives in shared spreadsheets that are not connected to the CRM	Check how the warranty list reaches the CRM, if at all, and how often
